{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head 301acdca44f76573505b28d1e562d684b0bab45d (tree 5745495aca553203f6ca3b8a9750efbd43819ef6), E2 (the MCP tool `explore` on a checked replica) over published main 536e2efc. The verdict binds the E2 implementation promise 1d028c9d on request a102d847, with reporting artifact 975f6420 (`packages/mcp/src/service.ts`), and signs all 27 path artifacts. No full gate passed at any head of this branch. The head qualifies for the limited follow-up in `docs/evidence-filing.md`, and the verdict rests on that rule, not on the request's conditions. Details below.\n\n## Evidence\n- **Artifacts.** The 27 filed artifacts equal the 27 changed paths (`git diff --no-renames --name-only 536e2efc 301acdca`): none missing, none extra, none twice. `gs review --prepare` binds the reporting artifact to its promise.\n- **Checks.** The merge into origin/main (536e2efc) is clean with tree 5745495a. `check-commit-messages` flags none of 8 commits, `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, `git diff --check` is clean. The added lines carry no other project's name and no `/Users/` path.\n- **Hashes.** 23 of the 25 distinct SHA-256 values in the implementation note match retained evidence files across the four evidence directories. The other two (`5e73d7c0…`, `e76cd829…`) are the before and after hashes of the generated Page JavaScript. They appear in `page-before.json` and `page-after.json`, which record each component.\n- **Seals.** The new `service.ts` module and `McpService` seals, the `hosted-tools.ts` whole-file seal and the new `explore-result.ts` seal sit in `scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs`. The entry-point inventory runs in the Node stage, which passed at 63c11ace (246 cases) on the same source and seal bytes.\n\n## Gate and follow-up eligibility\nBoth outcomes stand separately, as the rule requires.\n- **Full gate: failed.** END 1e9b5bb3 on 63c11ace (tree b9630b34): exit 1, 428.81 s. `gate.txt` (SHA-256 `c40a315b…`) names that head and tree with zero changed files. Static checks, typecheck, definition pins, workspace pins, kernel, Node 246 and Counting 60 passed. Plain ended with 5,094 passed, one skipped and one failed: `|client| test/replica.test.ts \u003e the package entry exports no test seam`. The Plain summary reads \"1 failed | 442 passed (443)\" test files. Native was unreached.\n- **Limited follow-up: passed.** END 4e3ef68e on 79f374e3 (tree 0286f9c0): exit 0. Whole `replica.test.ts` 31 passed in 1.51 s (`replica.log`, `ded06229…`), the client test compiler context ran (`client-test-types.log` is empty, hash `e3b0c442…`, 0.49 s), and the unreached Native stage passed: exit 0, 566.83 s, 303 files passed and one skipped, 1,240 cases passed and two skipped (`native.log`, `0493022d…`), crossings 0 lost, 4 stale, 0 unowned (`0ba9101f…`). Scale stayed omitted.\n- **Eligibility.** I checked each condition of the rule against the repository.\n  1. The gate failed in one test file only.\n  2. The repair changes only that file: `git diff --name-only 63c11ace 79f374e3` lists `packages/client/test/replica.test.ts`, one line.\n  3. Production code, configuration, dependencies and shared helpers are unchanged between the two heads, and 301acdca changes two notes only.\n  4. No file imports `replica.test.ts`.\n  5. The repair keeps every case and the exact-inventory assertion. It adds `\"explore\"` to the expected export list, and `explore` is the intended new public export of `@generalbusiness/dap-client/scope`.\n  6. The whole owning file, the compiler context and the unreached stage ran.\n- **What this does not do.** The follow-up does not turn the failed gate into a pass. A reader who needs a single passing full-gate END at one head will find none. The precedent is E1 (follow-up db3519 under 5fc60537).\n\n## Wake rate\nE2 adds no alarm, retry or reschedule. The only timer is the 10 s response deadline, which `finally` clears. The wake floor is unchanged. Requests per room per hour are unchanged when nobody calls the tool. Each call reads the configuration twice, the inventory, the membership Summary, one session request when none is cached, three Summaries and at most 16 journal pages: about 25 requests at the most, with the page count bounded by the 16-page budget. One call runs at a time per service instance. No call rate limit is added beyond that single slot, so the existing read admission governs repeat calls. `explore` is the costliest read tool per call.\n\n## Client: replica and E1 parity\n- **Architecture.** `explore(replica, key, act, { session, requestedAt }, clock)` sits behind the replica's own exploration closure, so no caller receives state or `Own`. It reuses E1's body judge (`actExplore`) and `judgeExploreGrant` from derive, and it turns the session's standing into a grant list that Scope cannot record. The result names the standing, its source, the judgment clock and `authorityAge`. C4's private call and absent-grant behavior are unchanged: `changed` appears only when `authority` is present.\n- **Parity witness.** The client test uses a real native-judged Lending birth and session. It compares the explored release's effects with an accepted release (`expect(admitted.effects).toEqual(actual.receipt.effects)`). It also checks that custody counts and the store stay unchanged and that no reader appears in the result.\n\n## Standing and clock controls\n- **Security.** The call refuses (`authority-unavailable`) when the session differs from the one the read returned, when the standing is missing or does not match the membership, key and claims, when `requestedAt` is missing or later than the clock, or when the declared action cannot be resolved. A session whose standing lacks the action gives `refused / unauthorized`. A backward clock gives unavailable. A replica refresh during the call gives unavailable. A closed replica gives unavailable. The test exercises each. `authorityAge` is a local elapsed-time bound, and the docs and note say it proves neither unchanged membership nor remote clock skew. The scope judges every real submit with its own read.\n- **Control.** `control.log` (`92111b65…`) shows that removing the checked grant makes the native-judged admission fail while the cache test still passes.\n\n## Exploration ownership on cancellation\n- **Architecture.** One slot per `McpService`. It releases only when the work has settled, the call is done, no physical fetch or body read is pending and no cancellation rejected. A rejected late cancellation keeps the slot closed for that instance. The 10 s deadline returns `unavailable` but does not free the slot.\n- **Security.** Logical journal input is bounded to 4 MiB and 16 pages, counted before the replica folds a page. A missing or oversized changed row, an incomplete `changed` list and an oversize envelope all return unavailable (`exploreData` and the final `bytesOf` check). The tool signs and submits nothing and writes no authorization. The target must be a listed declared scope, the act kind must be an admitted act of that scope and the input is closed. A service restricted to a listed set of tools does not expose it: `read-tools.test.ts` expects `unknown-tool` for `explore`, as for `room`.\n- **Witnesses.** Two owner tests with fake timers: a parallel call cannot enter configuration, and a fetch that ignores cancellation leaves the slot closed after the deadline, after release and after a rejected cancel (calls stay constant). The projection test refuses an incomplete or oversized delta. The budget test refuses excess bytes and a seventeenth page. A native Lending test explores a real release without an act write, checks the head is unchanged and returns `unavailable` when an independent release moves the head after the log read.\n\n## MCP inventory and documentation\n`GENERIC_TOOL_NAMES` gains `explore` (twenty-one names), and the hosted default policy lists it. `docs/mcp-tools.json` adds one entry and leaves the others unchanged. `docs/mcp.md` carries the exact description and schema. `docs/security/entry-points.md` states the owner, the bounds and the closed-slot rule, and lists `explore-result.ts` among the gate owners. The pins stage passed at 63c11ace. The regenerated Page asset (`apps/room/page/assets.ts`) changes the generated JavaScript hash only through minified identifier allocation: the HTML, inline CSS and OAuth JavaScript hashes are unchanged, and the pins reproduction passed.\n\n## Architecture\nThe tool is a read-only prediction over an authenticated journal. It uses one checked cut: first Summary, judgment head and final Summary must agree, the scope reference and definition digest must match, the host configuration digest must be unchanged and the same session must remain held and unexpired at the end. Any mismatch is `unavailable`. The note states what it does not prove.\n\n## Security\nNo new route, no `ScopeApi` method, no recorded grant and no derive export. The session token never enters the result (the client test checks the reader string is absent). Fail-closed on every limit. The tool is reachable on the hosted endpoint by default, behind the same OAuth and session as `room` and `inspect`.\n\n## Simplification\nOne client export, one MCP tool, one helper file and one owner slot. The `#readsOf` change names the read-session cache the tool shares. No new table or abstraction.\n\n## Deployment\nThe MCP hosted Agent and the room Worker (page assets) change. No Durable Object source, schema or table changes, so rollback is safe. Read the DO rate after the deploy (`scripts/do-rate.mjs`).\n\n## Non-blocking\n1. **Step table says \"Built in this head\".** `notes/2026-11-14-act-environment.md` marks E2 \"Built in this head\" in the step table. After landing, \"this head\" points at nothing. The Status line already says \"E2 added\", so the fix is one sentence. The planner's XD-1 landing required the same fix before land-now.\n2. **Compiler log has no exit record.** `client-test-types.log` is empty and its time file records no exit code. The note says exit 0. The follow-up change is a string in a test array, so the risk is nil. Recording the exit code would make the row checkable.\n3. **One failed fetch closes the slot for the instance.** After a rejected cancellation, `explore` returns `unavailable` until the service instance restarts. That is an availability trade, stated in the docs. Watch for it after the deploy.\n4. **Native Lending witness predates the cancellation repair.** The note says so, and the passing Native stage on 79f374e covers the final source.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:975f6420303e4d8bfe70d61ef0f632140d4558aa","binding":"assigned","head":"301acdca44f76573505b28d1e562d684b0bab45d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7746fc99a14b65ea4260e5326f8ce775ac3a397f\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1d028c9df6d8938bdbe23c6052e44b72e4c88497\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6416c96aec5e47ba51de6827ab8df7f08057bff4","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}